17.103-1 Policy

L1:(a) Except for DoD, NASA, and the Coast Guard, the contracting officer may enter into a multiyear contract if the head of the contracting activity determines that—
L2:(1) The need for the supplies or services is reasonably firm and continuing throughout the contract period; and
L2:(2) A multiyear contract will serve the best interest of the Government by either encouraging competition or promoting economy in program administration, performance, and operation.
L1:(b) For DoD, NASA, and the Coast Guard, the head of the agency may enter into a multiyear contract for supplies if—
L2:(1) The contract will save significant costs compared to using annual contracts, or will provide necessary stability to the defense industrial base not otherwise achievable through annual contracts;
L2:(2) The required purchase amounts are expected to remain substantially unchanged throughout the contract period, in terms of production and procurement rates and total quantities;
L2:(3) The agency head is reasonably expected to request sufficient funding throughout the contract period at a level required to avoid contract cancellation;
L2:(4) The design of the supplies is stable, and technical risks are not excessive;
L2:(5) Both the contract cost estimates and projected cost avoidance through the use of a multiyear contract are realistic;
L2:(6) For Department of Defense purchases, the contract will promote national security of the United States; and
L2:(7) For Department of Defense contracts valued at $500,000,000 or more, the Secretary certifies that the conditions in 10 U.S.C. 3501 (i)(3) subparagraphs (C) through (F) will be met.

17.204-1 Exercise of options

L1:(a) If the contract includes an economic price adjustment clause and the contractor requests a price revision, the contracting officer must determine how the adjustment affects option prices before exercising the option.
L1:(b) The contracting officer may exercise options only after—
L2:(1) Providing written notice to the contractor of the Government’s intention to exercise the option, within the time specified in the contract;
L2:(2) Confirming that funds are available; and
L2:(3) Determining that–
L3:(i) The option fulfills an existing Government requirement;
L3:(ii) The option was synopsized as required, unless exempt (see part 5);
L3:(iii) The Contractor does not have an active exclusion record in the System for Award Management (see part 9);
L3:(iv) The contractor’s performance on the current contract has been acceptable (for example, satisfactory or better performance ratings);
L3:(v) After reviewing price and other factors, the option price is fair and reasonable based on current market conditions and exercise of the option is in the Government's best interest; and
L3:(vi) The option exercise complies with the requirements of part 6 for full and open competition, i.e., the option was evaluated as part of the initial competition and is exercisable at an amount specified in or reasonably determinable from the terms of the contract.

17.702-2 Limitations

L1:(a) Management and operating contracts must not be authorized for—
L2:(1) Functions involving direction, supervision, or control of Government personnel, except supervision related to training;
L2:(2) Functions involving the exercise of police or regulatory powers in the Government's name, other than guard or plant protection services;
L2:(3) Functions determining basic Government policies;
L2:(4) Day-to-day staff or management functions of the agency or any of its elements; or
