45.000 Scope of part

L1:(a) This part prescribes policies and procedures for providing Government property to contractors; contractors’ management and use of Government property; and reporting, redistributing, and disposing of contractor inventory.
L1:(b) It does not apply to—
L2:(1) Government property provided under any statutory leasing authority, except as to non-Government use of property under 45.301(f);
L2:(2) Property to which the Government has acquired a lien or title solely because of partial, advance, progress, or performance based payments;
L2:(3) Disposal of real property;
L2:(4) Software and intellectual property; or
L2:(5) Government property that is incidental to the place of performance, when the contract requires contractor personnel to be located on a Government site or installation, and when the property used by the contractor within the location remains accountable to the Government. Items considered to be incidental to the place of performance include, for example, office space, desks, chairs, telephones, computers, and fax machines.

45.103 General

L1:(a) Agencies must—
L2:(1) Allow and encourage contractors to use voluntary consensus standards (see FAR part 11) and industry-leading practices and standards to manage Government property in their possession;
L2:(2) Eliminate to the maximum practical extent any competitive advantage a prospective contractor may have by using Government property;
L2:(3) Ensure maximum practical reutilization of contractor inventory for government purposes;
L2:(4) Require contractors to use Government property already in their possession to the maximum extent practical in performing Government contracts;
L2:(5) Charge appropriate rentals when the property is authorized for use on other than a rent-free basis; and
L2:(6) Require contractors to justify retaining Government property not needed for contract performance and to declare property as excess when no longer needed for contract performance.
L1:(b) Agencies will not generally require contractors to establish property management systems that are separate from a contractor’s established procedures, practices, and systems used to account for and manage contractor-owned property.

